{
 "table": "occupants",
 "title": "Her first-class passengers by stateroom: the Cave list (1912)",
 "format": 1,
 "written": "2026-11-24",
 "source": {
  "title": "First Class Passenger List, S.S. \"Titanic\", from Southampton and Cherbourg to New York (via Queenstown), Wednesday, 10th April, 1912 (\"Third Proof\"): the Cave list",
  "urls": [
   "https://archives.novascotia.ca/titanic/archives/?ID=1",
   "https://archives.novascotia.ca/titanic/fatalities/archives/?ID=218",
   "https://encyclopedia-titanica.org/the-cave-list.html",
   "https://hbiostat.org/data/repo/titanic3.csv"
  ],
  "what": "A \"Third Proof\" of the White Star Line's printed first-class passenger list for the maiden voyage, with cabin numbers set against the names, found on the body of saloon steward Herbert Cave (body no. 218, one of the 306 recovered by the cable ship Mackay-Bennett; buried at Fairview Cemetery, Halifax). The original is held by the Nova Scotia Archives (Medical Examiner, City of Halifax, RG 41 vol. 76 no. 218); its six list pages run alphabetically from Allen to Young in two columns, first-class passengers and their servants, with cabins mostly on decks A to E.",
  "transcribed_from": "scan: the Nova Scotia Archives' digitised original, read from the full-resolution Zoomify images of its pages 1-6 (pages 7-8 are White Star advertisements, 9 is blank, 10 is the back). Encyclopedia Titanica's transcription refused automated fetches and its archived copy could not be fetched, so it was not read. The cross-check is titanic3 (hbiostat.org, compiled from Encyclopedia Titanica), whose first-class cabins agree with this reading for most entries; this file's counts equal those Encyclopedia Titanica gives for the list (223 names, 25 servants, 157 cabins).",
  "accessed": "2026-11-24",
  "gaps": "Incomplete, being a proof: Encyclopedia Titanica counts 84 first-class passengers and servants who sailed but are not on it (among them the Astors, J. Bruce Ismay, the Ryersons and the Futrelles), while some it lists did not sail or sailed second class (noted per entry). 14 names in 12 entries have no cabin (Thomas Andrews, W. T. Stead and the Thayers among them), and 25 servants appear only as \"and Maid\", \"and Manservant\" or \"and Nurse\". Nothing is struck out or corrected by hand; two tears take one deck letter (Snyder) and the end of one name (Van der Hoef).",
  "conventions": "One entry per unbraced name, or per brace: the list joins name lines with a brace and sets one or more cabin numbers against it, without saying who in the brace had which cabin (a number sometimes stands level with a single line). cabin: the numbers in printed order, joined by \", \"; /1 /2 /3 are as printed (berths); square brackets mark letters lost in a tear and supplied. names: one per printed name, with its wrapped lines and \"and Maid\" joined to it; spelling and punctuation as printed, spacing normalised, line-break hyphens joined. Misspellings kept as printed include Aubert (Aubart), Buckell (Bucknell), Christy, Miss Juli (Julie), Earnshew (Earnshaw), Frauenthal T. G. (I. G.), Giglo (Giglio), Goldenberg E. L. (S. L.), Molsom (Molson) and Penchen (Peuchen). where: list page 1-6, col 1 left and col 2 right, lines counted from the top of the column (wrapped lines counted, letter headings not). note: damage, and where titanic3 differs."
 },
 "counts": {
  "entries": 158,
  "cabin_numbers_printed": 175,
  "cabins_distinct_as_printed": 162,
  "cabins_distinct_rooms": 157,
  "name_lines": 223,
  "unnamed_servants": 25,
  "entries_without_cabin": 12,
  "name_lines_without_cabin": 14
 },
 "rules": [
  "One row is one line of the list: its stateroom or staterooms (cabin) and its names, as the list writes them, in the list's order (n), with where in the list it stands. Nothing is normalised: a name stands as the list spells it.",
  "The list is first class's alone. Second and third class stay unnamed in her rooms: no list of their staterooms is used here.",
  "A line names the room it was allocated, which is not proof the passenger slept there that night: the source's gaps say what it leaves out."
 ],
 "how": [
  "labels and rooms are derived: node tools/occupants_table.js --write (labels by parseCabin from cabin, rooms by core/readers/cabins.js's index of R3's tables). A row with labels_note keeps labels read by hand, with its reason.",
  "core/readers/cabins.js puts each line's names on the rows its staterooms stand in (each row's people) and finds them by name (named). A line whose staterooms her plans do not number is kept apart (I.people.unplaced), with why.",
  "node tools/cabins_tests.js holds it: every line its fields, its labels and rooms derived, placed or kept apart with why, every name found in its own room, the index otherwise unchanged."
 ],
